"""Readable, topic-specific lesson help with original worked examples and sources."""
from html import escape

SOURCES = {
 "book": ('Jettelly — Visualizing Equations, Vol. 1', 'https://jettelly.com/store/visualizing-equations-vol-1'),
 "mit": ('MIT OpenCourseWare — Gilbert Strang, Linear Algebra', 'https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06sc-linear-algebra-fall-2011/'),
 "visual": ('3Blue1Brown — Essence of Linear Algebra', 'https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab'),
 "games": ('Essential Math for Games Programmers', 'https://www.essentialmath.com/'),
 "ik": ('Autodesk — IK handle', 'https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/ENU/MayaCRE-Tech-Docs/CommandsPython/ikHandle.html'),
 "point": ('Autodesk — point constraint', 'https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/ENU/MayaCRE-Tech-Docs/CommandsPython/pointConstraint.html'),
 "orient": ('Autodesk — orient constraint', 'https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/ENU/MayaCRE-Tech-Docs/CommandsPython/orientConstraint.html'),
 "parent": ('Autodesk — parent constraint', 'https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/ENU/MayaCRE-Tech-Docs/CommandsPython/parentConstraint.html'),
}

def ink(text,color):
    return '<b style="color:'+color+'">'+text+'</b>'
A=ink('a','#65c9ba'); B=ink('b','#e0bc60'); H=ink('H','#c2acd9')
PAGES={
'View':('Choose a teaching view',
'<p><b>Ortho (flat XY)</b> looks directly at the XY plane: X runs horizontally and Y vertically. It removes perspective and angled foreshortening for the early vector examples. Z depth is collapsed in this view.</p><p><b>Ortho (angled 3D)</b> reveals depth without perspective size changes. <b>Normal camera (perspective)</b> provides a conventional three-dimensional camera view.</p><p><b>Lesson default</b> starts vector, dot, and projection examples flat. Cross products use an angled view so their out-of-plane normal remains visible. Transform and orientation examples use an angled perspective view; position interpolation uses an angled orthographic view.</p><p><b>Frame view</b> fits the current example using the selected view. Choosing a view changes only the camera. An explicit choice remains selected as you change steps.</p>',()),
'Step 1: S to E':('Step 1: one displacement',
'<p>Start with two positions: S is the start and E is the end. The teal arrow is the displacement a = E − S.</p><p>S = (0,0,0), E = (3,4,0): a = (3−0,4−0,0−0) = (3,4,0).</p><p>Move S or E and watch the subtraction update. Choose Normalize to introduce length and unit direction, or Vectors to add W and a second displacement.</p>',('book','visual')),
'Vectors':('Positions and displacements',
'<p>A point tells you <b>where</b>. A displacement tells you <b>how far and in which direction to move</b>. S, E and W are points. E connects two arrows; it is not necessarily their midpoint.</p>'
'<p>'+ink('S = (0,0,0)','#ff8888')+' &nbsp; '+ink('E = (3,4,0)','#8ddd96')+' &nbsp; '+ink('W = (6,0,0)','#8fbaff')+'</p>'
'<h3>Formula → substitution → result</h3><p>'+A+' = E − S = (3−0, 4−0, 0−0) = (3,4,0)</p><p>'+B+' = W − E = (6−3, 0−4, 0−0) = (3,−4,0)</p><p>'+A+' + '+B+' = (6,0,0) = W − S</p>'
'<h3>Look in the viewport</h3><p>Teal runs S→E; gold runs E→W; green joins S→W. The green arrow is the sum. Coordinates are world-space centimeters.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Move E while leaving S and W fixed. Each segment changes, but their sum stays W−S. Translate all points together: world positions change; displacement vectors do not.</p>',('book','visual','mit')),
'Normalize':('Length and unit direction',
'<p>Length answers “how far?” Normalization keeps only direction.</p><h3>Calculate</h3><p>'+A+'=(3,4,0)</p><p>|a| = √(3²+4²+0²) = 5 cm</p><p>'+ink('u','#8fbaff')+' = a/|a| = (0.6,0.8,0); |u|=1</p><h3>Look and try</h3><p>The blue unit arrow has the teal arrow’s direction. Move E twice as far from S: length doubles; unit direction stays the same. Put E on S: length becomes zero and normalization is undefined. The tool hides the undefined arrow rather than inventing a direction.</p><p>Use a unit direction for aim or movement; multiply by speed and elapsed time to obtain a displacement.</p>',('book','visual','games')),
'Dot / projection':('Dot product versus projection',
'<p>A dot product returns a <b>scalar</b>. A projection can use that scalar to build a vector and a point. Do not give all three the same name.</p><h3>Segment alignment</h3><p>'+A+'·'+B+' = 3×3 + 4×(−4) + 0×0 = −7</p><p>cos θ = −7/(5×5) = −0.28; θ ≈ 106.26°</p><p>This is the angle between the arrow directions. The interior elbow angle is 180°−θ, approximately 73.74°.</p><h3>Project E onto the S–W line</h3><p>g = W−S = (6,0,0); u = g/|g| = (1,0,0)</p><p>k = a·u = 3 cm</p><p>'+H+' = S + p = S + k u = (3,0,0)</p><p>'+ink('p = H−S','#c2acd9')+' = (3,0,0) is the purple projected displacement.</p><p>'+ink('r = E−H','#ef95cd')+' = (0,4,0) is the pink perpendicular residual.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Move E across the guide. Watch the residual reverse. H can lie beyond S or W: this is an infinite-line projection, not a clamped segment. If S=W, the guide direction is undefined.</p>',('book','mit','visual')),
'Cross / plane':('Cross product: a perpendicular vector',
'<p>The result c is perpendicular to a and b. Its magnitude measures parallelogram area; normalizing c produces a direction n. Colors distinguish the two operands; axis letters identify the output component.</p><h3>Formula first</h3><p>c = a × b</p><p>cₓ = aᵧb_z − a_zbᵧ<br>cᵧ = a_zbₓ − aₓb_z<br>c_z = aₓbᵧ − aᵧbₓ</p><h3>Then substitute</h3><p>'+A+'=(3,4,0), '+B+'=(3,−4,0)</p><p>'+ink('cₓ = 4×0 − 0×(−4) = 0','#ff8888')+'<br>'+ink('cᵧ = 0×3 − 3×0 = 0','#8ddd96')+'<br>'+ink('c_z = 3×(−4) − 4×3 = −24','#8fbaff')+'</p><p>c=(0,0,−24); |c|=24 cm²</p><p>n=c/|c|=(0,0,−1)</p><h3>Look and try</h3><p>The normal arrow is drawn 3× longer only for readability. It is not the elbow pole: the normal is perpendicular to the bend plane, while the pole helps choose that plane. Swap a and b: c reverses. Make them parallel: c=0 and a unique unit normal does not exist.</p>',('book','visual','games')),
'Control mode':('IK, FK and the bend plane',
'<p><b>IK:</b> move the wrist target to choose the end position. Move the pink pole to choose the bend plane. The elbow-to-pole guide is a relationship line, not the cross-product normal.</p><p><b>FK:</b> rotate the shoulder, elbow and wrist in order. The wrist rotates the hand without changing the two bone lengths. Switching modes matches the current pose.</p><h3>Reach</h3><p>L₁=5 cm, L₂=5 cm. Reachable target distance r satisfies |L₁−L₂| ≤ r ≤ L₁+L₂, so 0 ≤ r ≤ 10 cm.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Move the target beyond 10 cm. The chain cannot reach it without stretching. Bring it back, then move only the pole. Observe that choosing an end position is not enough to choose a bend plane.</p>',('ik','games')),
'Link':('Holding a prop: what does a constraint drive?',
'<p>The prop starts immediately beneath the palm. Its local axes show orientation; its label names the active link. Switching links preserves the current pose by recording an offset.</p><table cellpadding="8"><tr><th>Link</th><th>Follows wrist</th><th>Free to edit</th></tr><tr><td>Point</td><td>Position</td><td>Rotation</td></tr><tr><td>Orient</td><td>Rotation</td><td>Position</td></tr><tr><td>Parent constraint</td><td>Position + rotation</td><td>Neither driven channel</td></tr><tr><td>Hierarchy parent</td><td>Parent transform, including scale</td><td>Local offsets</td></tr></table><h3>Predict, then test</h3><p>Choose Orient and move the wrist. The prop need not stay in the hand because position is independent. Choose Parent constraint for a held prop. To change its offset, detach to World, edit, then relink.</p><p>The math panel reports native constraint offsets and evaluated outputs. It does not claim that adding Euler angles reproduces Maya’s full orientation solver.</p>',('point','orient','parent')),
'Interpolation':('Animate between two orientations',
'<p><b>t</b> is progress: 0=start, 0.5=halfway, 1=end. The separated reference boxes show endpoint orientations; their spacing is not position interpolation.</p><h3>Euler LERP</h3><p>e(t)=(1−t)e₀+t e₁. For XYZ endpoints (0,0,0) and (90,90,0), t=0.5 gives (45,45,0) degrees.</p><h3>Quaternion SLERP</h3><p>q=(x,y,z,w) is a unit quaternion. xyz=axis×sin(angle/2); w=cos(angle/2). This w is neither a point named W nor a homogeneous coordinate.</p><p>ω=acos(q₀·q₁). q(t)=sin((1−t)ω)/sin(ω) q₀ + sin(tω)/sin(ω) q₁.</p><p>For these endpoints, q₀=(0,0,0,1), q₁=(0.5,0.5,−0.5,0.5). At t=0.5, q≈(0.2887,0.2887,−0.2887,0.8660). The intermediate orientation differs from Euler LERP even though the endpoints agree.</p><h3>Play and compare</h3><p>Play preview sweeps t from 0 to 1 over four seconds. Compare each method. SLERP follows the shortest rotation arc at constant angular speed when t changes linearly. Manual rotation changes the actual pose; changing t reapplies the formula.</p><h3>Bake for Maya playback</h3><p>Bake creates a separate pair of keyed boxes: Euler on the left, SLERP on the right. Use the main Maya viewport and timeline. Samples are exact at keyed frames; linear Euler curves between samples approximate SLERP. The take survives closing the tool and can be saved with the scene. Reset lesson does not delete separate takes.</p>',('book','games')),
'Spaces':('One point, different coordinate frames',
'<p>Local coordinates describe a point relative to an object. World coordinates describe that same point relative to the scene frame.</p><h3>Maya’s row-vector convention</h3><p>p_world = p_local M_world</p><p>For translation (10,20,30): (3,4,0,1)M=(13,24,30,1).</p><p>A direction uses w=0: (3,4,0,0)M=(3,4,0,0). Translation has no effect on it.</p><h3>Try it</h3><p>Rotate the parent and inspect world components. Local geometry stays unchanged. A normal under nonuniform scale needs inverse-transpose handling; it is not generally transformed like an ordinary direction.</p>',('mit','visual','games')),
}

def for_topic(topic=None):
    aliases={"Lecture step":"Step 1: S to E", "Variation":"Step 1: S to E", "Dot / alignment":"Dot / projection","Example":"Vectors","Rig only":"Control mode","Orientation interpolation":"Interpolation","Lesson":"Vectors"}
    selected=aliases.get(topic,topic)
    keys=[selected] if selected in PAGES else list(PAGES)
    pieces=['<h1>Week 5 · Math in Maya</h1><p>Read the formula, predict a result, change one input, then compare the viewport.</p>']
    for key in keys:
        title,body,refs=PAGES[key]
        pieces.append('<h2>'+escape(title)+'</h2>'+body+'<h3>Read / watch next</h3><ul>'+''.join('<li><a href="'+SOURCES[r][1]+'">'+SOURCES[r][0]+'</a></li>' for r in refs)+'</ul>')
    if selected == 'Interpolation' or topic is None:
        pieces.append('<p><b>Quaternion symbols:</b> q₀ and q₁ are endpoint orientations. ω is the angle between them in quaternion space: half the physical rotation angle for this example. The XYZ endpoint angles use Maya’s XYZ rotation order. At t=0.5, switch methods to compare the halfway pose.</p>')
    if selected == 'Vectors' or topic is None:
        pieces.append('<p><b>Examples:</b> presets load starting values. Moving a point marks the example Modified; the preset name no longer describes the current geometry. Restore example returns to its known values. S is the tail of a, E joins a and b, and W is the head of b.</p>')
    pieces.append('<p><b>Controls:</b> Reset rebuilds the current lesson. Restore example restores its points. Frame changes only the view. Save writes the viewport and worked math. Hover over an action for its behavior; use ? for the current topic. Undefined inputs remain visible in the math display.</p>')
    return '<html><head><style>a {color:#8bc8ff;} h2 {color:#b9d783;} h3 {color:#c2acd9;} td,th {background:#363636;}</style></head><body style="background:#292929;color:#ededed;font-family:Segoe UI;font-size:13pt;line-height:1.5">'+''.join(pieces)+'</body></html>'

HTML=for_topic()
